WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.723
Picture a small bank with two accounts, A and B, and 1,000 in each.

2
00:00:07.723 --> 00:00:09.659
Together, they hold 2,000.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.928
A transfer moves 50 from A to B in two steps.

4
00:00:12.928 --> 00:00:14.805
First it takes 50 out of A.

5
00:00:14.805 --> 00:00:16.264
Then it adds 50 to B.

6
00:00:16.264 --> 00:00:22.520
A thread does this work: a line of work that runs side by side with others in one program.

7
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:26.790
A second thread, the checker, adds up both accounts now and then.

8
00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:30.659
Money only moves around, so the total should always be 2,000.

9
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.747
But look between the two steps.

10
00:00:32.747 --> 00:00:35.699
The 50 has left A, and it hasn't reached B yet.

11
00:00:35.699 --> 00:00:38.777
It's in the transfer's hands, out in the hallway.

12
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:42.980
If the checker adds up right then, it gets 1,950.

13
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.200
Fifty missing, and nobody stole a cent.

14
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:50.052
We want nobody to see the books while a transfer is halfway done.

15
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.756
Banks solved that long ago with a vault.

16
00:00:52.756 --> 00:00:55.440
One room, one door, and one key on a hook.

17
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.427
Let's map it.

18
00:00:56.427 --> 00:00:59.990
The room is the code we guard, with the balances inside.

19
00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:03.552
The key is a lock: only the thread holding it may go in.

20
00:01:03.552 --> 00:01:08.960
It takes the key going in, and hangs it back going out, even if something goes wrong.

21
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:12.547
Any other thread that wants the key waits by the door.

22
00:01:12.547 --> 00:01:16.656
Java calls that state BLOCKED: alive, but not allowed to run yet.

23
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.804
In Java, the word for all this is synchronized.

24
00:01:19.804 --> 00:01:23.576
Every object has a built-in key, and synchronized takes it.

25
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.561
Now the question many of us are too shy to ask.

26
00:01:30.561 --> 00:01:33.908
The checker only reads, and reading never broke anything.

27
00:01:33.908 --> 00:01:35.905
So it doesn't need the key, right?

28
00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:37.038
Let's test it.

29
00:01:37.038 --> 00:01:41.688
The transfer moves 50 and back, 200,000 times, always with the key.

30
00:01:41.688 --> 00:01:45.505
The checker adds up as fast as it can, without the key.

31
00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.656
Will it ever see a wrong total?

32
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:56.131
In one run, the checker made about 41 million checks, and 2.8 million were wrong.

33
00:01:56.131 --> 00:01:58.424
That's about one check in fifteen.

34
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.807
Well, that's a lot of missing money.

35
00:02:00.807 --> 00:02:02.339
Here's what we got wrong.

36
00:02:02.339 --> 00:02:04.791
The lock guards the door, not the money.

37
00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:07.181
And the checker never came to the door.

38
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:10.541
In a real bank, you can't read the books from outside.

39
00:02:10.541 --> 00:02:12.501
But Java's vault has glass walls.

40
00:02:12.501 --> 00:02:15.887
Any thread can read straight through them, key or no key.

41
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:18.308
So the checker asks for the key too.

42
00:02:18.308 --> 00:02:22.480
Now it waits in line, and it only looks when no transfer is inside.

43
00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:27.505
Same test again: about 905,000 checks, and not one wrong total.

44
00:02:27.505 --> 00:02:31.715
Fewer checks, though, because each one now waits its turn.

45
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:32.808
Now the names.

46
00:02:32.808 --> 00:02:36.841
The built-in key on every Java object is called its monitor.

47
00:02:36.841 --> 00:02:38.320
It makes two promises.

48
00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.883
One thread inside at a time: that's mutual exclusion.

49
00:02:41.883 --> 00:02:46.791
And the next thread to take the key sees every change: that's visibility.

50
00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:50.661
But both promises go only to threads that take the same key.

51
00:02:50.661 --> 00:02:55.011
A lock protects only the threads that take it, so readers take it too.

52
00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.211
Next puzzle.

53
00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:04.432
An account must never go below zero, so withdraw first asks canCover: is there enough money?

54
00:03:04.432 --> 00:03:05.920
Both use the same key.

55
00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.165
So the thread holding the key asks for it again.

56
00:03:09.165 --> 00:03:10.788
Does it wait for itself?

57
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:18.799
If it did, it would wait forever, like searching the whole house for the glasses on your head.

58
00:03:18.933 --> 00:03:19.740
It doesn't.

59
00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:25.386
Java's monitors are reentrant: the thread holding the key just walks back in.

60
00:03:25.386 --> 00:03:26.779
Java keeps a count.

61
00:03:26.779 --> 00:03:28.173
Into withdraw, one.

62
00:03:28.173 --> 00:03:29.566
Into canCover, two.

63
00:03:29.566 --> 00:03:30.592
Back out, one.

64
00:03:30.592 --> 00:03:33.892
Then zero, and the key goes back on the hook.

65
00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:36.651
Which object should be the key?

66
00:03:36.651 --> 00:03:38.572
Not the text "lock" in quotes.

67
00:03:38.572 --> 00:03:43.950
Every class that writes "lock" shares that one object, like a key under the doormat.

68
00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:48.880
In the demo, a class that never meant to share waited about 900 milliseconds.

69
00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:50.970
Use a private final object.

70
00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.861
Private, so no other class can borrow it.

71
00:03:53.861 --> 00:03:55.764
Final, so it never changes.

72
00:03:56.533 --> 00:03:57.152
Last one.

73
00:03:57.152 --> 00:04:02.449
Before moving money, the bank asks a fraud check service on another computer.

74
00:04:02.449 --> 00:04:04.788
Each answer takes 40 milliseconds.

75
00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:09.228
Now picture a transfer in the vault with the key, phoning the fraud desk.

76
00:04:09.228 --> 00:04:13.288
It's on hold, with the key in its pocket, and the line outside grows.

77
00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:16.358
Eight threads make five transfers each.

78
00:04:16.358 --> 00:04:20.352
With the check inside the lock, how long do those forty checks take?

79
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.231
One thread at a time means one check at a time.

80
00:04:27.231 --> 00:04:29.676
Forty times 40 is about 1,600.

81
00:04:29.676 --> 00:04:32.692
The demo measured 1,618 milliseconds.

82
00:04:32.692 --> 00:04:39.050
Move the check outside the lock, and the checks overlap, five rounds of eight.

83
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.436
201 milliseconds.

84
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.700
So never go on hold with the key.

85
00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:44.800
Do the slow work before or after.

86
00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.236
Inside the lock, only read and write the shared state.

87
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.029
Here it is in code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

88
00:04:53.029 --> 00:04:54.483
One private final key.

89
00:04:54.483 --> 00:04:56.662
The slow check, with no key held.

90
00:04:56.662 --> 00:05:00.824
Then every read and write of the balances, under that same key.

91
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.582
So, does a reader need the key?

92
00:05:03.582 --> 00:05:03.837
Yes.

93
00:05:03.837 --> 00:05:07.928
Java's vault has glass walls, and the lock only guards the door.

94
00:05:07.928 --> 00:05:14.896
Give the books one private key, make every reader and writer take it, and never go on hold while you hold it.

95
00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:15.996
Quick check.

96
00:05:15.996 --> 00:05:20.878
The checker locks, but on its own private key, not the vault's.

97
00:05:20.878 --> 00:05:22.660
Can it still see 1,950?
